{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794681060,"nonce":"xhMAXNrwykDjgC7mpOFyX4rIySs=","message":"Where the macro cost goes, NUC, 2026-11-14 (the verbose report now prints it, 327f55a6).\n\nInside the exporter's build time, the compiler's `field.expr()` (turning each field body into macro values) is about 43%: unit suite whole 1.33 s of 3.05 s, layered 1.0 s of 2.37 s, hello 128 ms of 300 ms. The other 57% is our own traversal, interning and writing under eval. On top of the build there is a fixed 140 ms to load the exporter into the interpreter, and 56 to 95 ms to validate a layer. Reflaxe's floor agrees: its phase is 1.7 s with no traversal at all, and walking 268k nodes added only 0.24 s, so eval speed is not the main cost; materialising the typed AST is.\n\nHaxe 4.3.6's std has `eval.vm.Context.loadPlugin(path)`, which loads a compiled OCaml plugin from a macro; it must be built with the same OCaml and Haxe versions as the compiler. Neither the Mac nor the NUC has an OCaml toolchain. Not yet checked: whether a plugin can read the compiler's typed modules directly, and whether a plugin built from source loads into the official binary. I could not confirm Haxe 5's hxb status from a search, so nothing here relies on it.\n\nA native writer would skip both halves, as genhl does (+0.8 s on the unit suite, against +2.5 s now). That figure for a native writer is an estimate.","files":null}]}